  "travel_time_band": {
   "version": "0.3.0",
   "released": "2026-10-10",
   "changes": [
    {
     "kind": "initial",
     "description": "Narrows reachable junctions to a shorter travel time, recomputing the budget left at each against the band. The rings of an isochrone map -- 1, 5, 10 and 15 minutes in the case study -- are not four traversals: a cumulative band is a subset, and the notebook's own note says nested thresholds are cumulative rather than disjoint. isochrone recomputes the whole Dijkstra per threshold including the snap, four times over for the Soho example, where one traversal banded four ways gives the same answer. Not a generic attribute filter, and deliberately: reachable_corridor truncates streets by remainingM over lengthM, so keeping a five minute subset of a fifteen minute traversal without recomputing remainingM would leave the longer budget's leftovers in place and draw corridors past the band, silently. The filter and the recomputation have to happen together. Takes no zone and no speed: the speed arrives on the junctions and the traversal's budget is recovered from them."
    },
    {
     "kind": "added",
     "description": "Each junction kept now carries bandMinutes. reachable_corridor reads it to stamp the band's travel time onto the polygon it draws, so a map can colour by walking time and a chart can label by it, as the case study's outputs do. Without it the four rings arrive at a map indistinguishable from one another. Additive: no existing attribute changed."
    },
    {
     "kind": "added",
     "description": "Records the fw: terms this widget asserts, as ontology.emits, derived from its module by scripts/ontology-coupling.mjs. The point is the coupling rather than the list: the terms a widget asserts can no longer change without a new release, so a composition pinning the old one reports drift, and ontology drift and widget drift become one signal. Declares 1 class and 6 properties. No behaviour change."
    }
   ],
